  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">U.S. President&#x2019;s Malaria Initiative for States Task Order 4 (TO4)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Nigeria):  With FY 2022 funds this activity will:.Provide technical assistance, training, and small grants to local organizations to: 1) build capacity for the management of malaria prevention and case management in public and private health facilities; 2) prevent malaria in pregnancy, including providing intermittent preventive malaria therapy for pregnant women; 3) strengthen health systems for service delivery, including supervision and monitoring; and 4) improve reporting and quality of health data from service delivery points; and.Improve malaria case management and expand access to services that prevent malaria in pregnancy..Malaria contributes significantly to morbidity and mortality of children and pregnant women. The United States is a major partner in efforts to fight the malaria scourge in Nigeria which has a national average prevalence of 23 percent (Demographic and Health Survey, 2018). This activity implements in Zamfara, Nasarawa, Benue, and Plateau states, serving an estimated 18 million people. .This activity will lead to increased utilization of quality health services in target areas and population groups and contribute to USAID priorities by focusing on evidence-based interventions, sustainability, and capacity building. This activity contributes directly to USAID Nigeria CDCS Development Objective 2, the Embassy&apos;s ICS, and is closely aligned with Goal 3 of the 2022-2026 Joint Strategic Plan..</narrative>
  </description>
